An ACK taken over what quic-go's history holds, not over what ngtcp2_rtb holds.

quic-go keeps every packet it sent in its history until the packet is acknowledged or declared lost —
the ones that elicit no acknowledgement too — and a packet declared lost leaves it
(vendor/quic-go/internal/ackhandler/sent_packet_handler.go:331,
vendor/quic-go/internal/ackhandler/sent_packet_history.go:61-67, :230-246).
ReceivedAck works from that (vendor/quic-go/internal/ackhandler/sent_packet_handler.go:403-521). Upstream ngtcp2 worked from
ngtcp2_rtb, which keeps no ACK-only packet and keeps a lost one, and a client showed it:

  - an ACK acknowledging only ACK-only packets looked for no losses, so data packets three behind it
    waited for the timer or the next ACK; quic-go declares them lost with it;
  - an ACK of a packet already declared lost was processed as an acknowledgement: an RTT sample, an
    acknowledgement for the controller, and its frames taken as delivered, which cancelled the
    retransmission queued when it was lost. To quic-go the packet is gone and the ACK changes
    nothing; the retransmission goes out;
  - the largest acknowledged rose with every ACK, even one that acknowledged nothing; quic-go leaves
    it and does nothing at all (:424-427);
  - an ACK whose largest was an ACK-only packet gave no RTT sample; quic-go samples it when anything
    ack-eliciting was acknowledged with it, unless it was sent before the last sampled one
    (:430-449, largestAckedTime);
  - the loss timer was set from ngtcp2_rtb alone; quic-go sets it from the oldest packet up to the
    largest acknowledged, whichever kind (:881-888). And with nothing outstanding a client has no
    timer, whatever the loss time says: quic-go cancels the alarm before it looks (:761-766).

The ACK-only packets patch 0030 kept beside ngtcp2_rtb for the ECN tracker are kept per packet
number space now (pktns->tx.nae), in every space, and loss detection gives them up by quic-go's two
thresholds. The count ngtcp2_rtb_recv_ack returns is quic-go's: every packet newly acknowledged, so
the probe count, the PTO backoff and the handshake confirmation of patch 0028 follow it as quic-go's
do. The server keeps upstream's code.

Checked against quic-go's own sentPacketHandler, run by tools/qgovec: test/ngpatch.

